(11, 'Business', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/20/boeing-737-max-10-certification.html', 'Leslie Josephs', 'Boeing wins FAA approval for 737 Max 10 after years of delays as Duffy says certification process should be faster', 'Read more about aircraft delays', 'The Federal Aviation Administration on Tuesday signed off on the Boeing 737 Max 10 after years of delays, a major win for the manufacturer and the last in Boeing\'s best-selling family of jets to get government approval. The green light comes weeks after federal officials reviewed a navigation system glitch on the aircraft but deemed it was not a safety-of-flight issue. Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy told CNBC\'s Phil LeBeau that safety is paramount, but he also said the U.S. needs to improve its certification process. \"It takes Boeing, to certify an aircraft in Europe, about half the time, less than half the time that it does in the U.S. You have China now playing to take over in aviation,\" Duffy said. \"We have to certify aircraft more quickly if we\'re going to compete as Americans.\" The 737 Max 10, the largest model in the family that can seat up to 230 people, was set to start flying in 2020, but increased scrutiny following two Max crashes in 2018 and 2019 and other safety issues delayed its certification. Engineering changes were also required, including to an anti-icing system for the aircraft\'s engines. Max 10 customers include Alaska Airlines, American Airlines, United Airlines and Delta Air Lines. Boeing said the Max 10 makes up about 30% of its Max backlog. Airlines can finally start flying passengers soon after planes are certified, but it can take months for carriers to work them into schedules. \"Completing the certification program clears the path for us to deliver a Boeing airplane that will set a new standard in commercial aviation,\" Stephanie Pope, CEO of Boeing\'s commercial airplane unit, said in a news release. United CEO Scott Kirby told CNBC in August that the airline had been waiting so long for the Max 10 that the lie-flat seats it expected for the plane no longer make sense and are sitting in a warehouse. Alaska Airlines last month said it plans to put lie-flat seats on some of its 737 Max 10s in a push for more premium offerings. The FAA approved the smallest model of the 737 Max family, the Max 7, in early August. Boeing has been working to ramp up production of its 737 Maxes and 787 Dreamliners, which are key to its recovery since airlines and other customers pay the bulk of the aircraft\'s price upon delivery. The manufacturer opened a fourth production line in Everett, Washington, in July for its 737 Max airplanes.'),
(12, 'Business', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/20/general-motors-gm-earnings-q3-2026.html', 'Michael Wayland', 'General Motors beats Wall Street\'s Q3 earnings expectations, narrows full-year guidance', 'No headings found', 'DETROIT — General Motors narrowed its 2026 earnings guidance and raised its free cash flow expectations after beating Wall Street\'s third-quarter expectations. The Detroit automaker also announced a new multiyear stock buyback of $20 billion after GM completed a $6 billion share repurchase program that was announced in January. GM did not disclose a specific time to complete the program. Despite the positive news, GM stock fell nearly 7% during trading Tuesday as the company\'s outlook missed Wall Street\'s expectations. Wall Street analysts were mixed before the report on whether GM would raise its guidance Tuesday amid concerns of slowing sales and rising commodity costs. Analysts\' expectations included a midpoint of $13.41 per share for the year compared with GM\'s new $13.25 per share midpoint, according to LSEG. Several analysts in investor notes Tuesday said GM\'s third-quarter results were solid, but raised concerns about whether higher costs and pressures on vehicle pricing would limit earnings growth next year. Here\'s how GM performed in the third quarter, compared with average estimates compiled by LSEG: GM\'s new guidance includes adjusted earnings before interest and taxes of between $14.5 billion and $15.5 billion, or $12.75 to $13.75 adjusted earnings per share, and adjusted automotive free cash flow of between $11.5 billion and $12.5 billion. That compares with its raised guidance from July that included adjusted EBIT of between $14 billion and $16 billion, or $12 to $14 adjusted EPS, and adjusted automotive free cash flow of $9.5 billion to $11.5 billion. \"I think the market\'s been pretty consistent from what we\'ve said all year,\" GM CFO Paul Jacobson told CNBC\'s Phil LeBeau. \"Our fourth quarter\'s going to be down sequentially. ... I think the fact that we\'re able to hold our guidance in the face of that late inflationary pressure in the year is another sign of the GM team executing really well.\" GM on Tuesday said it expects commodity inflation costs to be around $2 billion, which was the high end of its previous guidance. That includes higher costs for logistics and dynamic random-access memory, or DRAM, chips that are impacting multiple industries. While the inflationary costs were at the automaker\'s high end of guidance, GM said its gross tariff costs are expected to be at the low end of its expected $2.5 billion to $3.5 billion range. Warranty costs are expected to be at high end between $1 billion and $1.5 billion, GM said. Jacobson said vehicle pricing is relatively flat as inflationary costs continue to increase. He said the company would assess where consumers are in the coming year when deciding if it should pass costs along by raising prices more than normal. \"GM is performing well, and we are working hard to continue our momentum despite growing inflationary pressures through strategic investments in new products and services, and through improved quality and operating efficiency,\" GM CEO Mary Barra said Tuesday in a letter to shareholders. The automaker also said the costs of its pullback in all-electric vehicles announced last year were \"substantially completed,\" saying it has recorded $11.2 billion in such charges since the second half of last year, including paying $4.6 billion of $7.4 billion in cash through the third quarter. The company\'s net income attributed to stockholders was $3.3 billion during the third quarter, up roughly $2 billion from a year earlier. GM\'s North American operations continued to lead the automaker\'s results, accounting for $3.6 billion of its $4.1 billion in adjusted EBIT results during the third quarter. GM\'s 2025 third-quarter results included $48.59 billion in revenue, net income attributable to stockholders of $1.3 billion and earnings per share of $2.80 adjusted.'),
(13, 'Business', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/16/nike-india-campus-china-sales.html', 'Laya Neelakandan', 'Nike\'s India campus is a long-term growth bet amid turnaround', 'Investing in a long-term vision | A growing market opportunity', 'Nike is making a big bet on India. The retailer, which has been struggling to revive its business and regain momentum under CEO Elliott Hill, is hoping the world\'s most populous country can help it turn the page on its performance. As it reported its fiscal first-quarter earnings on Oct. 1, the company announced it will create a new global campus in Bengaluru, India, as part of its growth strategy. Hill wrote in a letter to employees that the new facility would be a \"long-term investment\" to help position Nike for success. \"India is already an important growth market and manufacturing hub for Nike, with strong capabilities and access to talent,\" Hill wrote. The new campus is expected to open in the next few years and span corporate roles across the organization, including the namesake Nike brand, Jordan Brand and Converse. Nike plans to discuss its India campus in detail at its investor day next month. In a statement to CNBC, a Nike spokesperson said expanding into India is a \"natural\" next step for the company as it looks to grow its global presence. \"As participation in running, basketball, football, fitness and other sports continues to grow across India, we see an opportunity to better serve athletes through stronger local capabilities, consumer insights, and innovation,\" the spokesperson said. India will be the world\'s third-largest retail market by 2030, according to Indian conglomerate Reliance Industries. Its e-commerce market was valued at $159.25 billion in 2026 and is projected to reach $332.94 billion by 2031, according to IBEF, a business advocacy organization created by the Indian government. Nike has endured a yearslong slump and is trying to right its business under Hill\'s turnaround plan. Nike also announced this month that it would lay off workers across the company as part of what it has dubbed its Pace strategy, which includes supply chain changes and reorganization of the company\'s geographic segments. Nike expects the effort to deliver approximately $2.5 billion in savings through fiscal 2031. Nike\'s decision to invest further in India as part of its comeback comes as the sportswear company sheds revenue in another critical international market: China. Though its fiscal first-quarter North America revenue came in just above Wall Street expectations, Hill said that performance wasn\'t enough to offset pressures abroad. Nike has been particularly struggling in the Greater China market, where its revenue dropped by 26% in its fiscal first quarter. It\'s part of what experts have said is a broader trend of American companies losing relevance and traction to domestic brands as the once-lucrative market matures. On a call with analysts earlier this month, Hill said the company was taking \"deliberate actions\" to strengthen its China business, though he said those effects will take time to materialize. Hill said the company plans to refresh its physical retail, spearhead a digital cleanup and create higher quality experiences for consumers. The Nike spokesperson told CNBC the company is \"deeply committed\" to China and sees the region as one of its \"most important long-term growth markets,\" even as Nike expands its presence in India. \"China remains a long-term priority for Nike, while India represents another important opportunity to invest in the future of sport and the next generation of athletes,\" the spokesperson said. The company\'s expansion in India may be coming at both an opportune and inopportune time, according to experts. Investing in a growing international market does not guarantee higher sales — it also requires persuading Indian consumers they want the Nike brand, a challenge the company has struggled to overcome in China. \"What Nike, I think, is doing right now is going region by region and working to reestablish what the brand stands for,\" Simeon Siegel, a senior managing director at Guggenheim Securities, told CNBC. Siegel said part of the dissonance for Nike is that each region and segment of the business is at a different stage of its turnaround. He said investing in India may set the company up for a more sustainable future if the China business continues to lag, but Nike needs to have a compelling product, solid storytelling and extensive distribution in the region to be successful there. \"I think the company needs to look inward and figure out what is the right way to reestablish profitability,\" he said. \"And that involves, in certain instances, shrinking and saving and cutting, and, in certain instances, investing and looking toward the future, because the goal here is not to shrink to oblivion; it\'s to shrink to stability, and then ultimately growth.\" The global strategy may also focus too much on a long-term vision rather than more immediate fixes, according to Jessica Ramirez, co-founder and managing director at advisory firm The Consumer Collective, who specializes in retail and apparel. Ramirez said she already thought Nike and Hill\'s vision were \"out of touch\" and the plan for the India campus didn\'t necessarily help its case. \"I don\'t know that this plan of India is an immediate solution to their problems right now,\" she said. \"They have to plan long term, right? But they also need to plan short term, which is continuously falling apart.\" She said Nike has not shared plans that are concrete enough to win back momentum in China and has to develop both regions hand in hand. While Nike under Hill has improved its wholesale and performance categories, Ramirez said it has yet to master its direct-to-consumer and sportswear segments, which would allow the company to flourish in all of its markets. The Nike spokesperson told CNBC the retailer is committed to investing in both countries as it tries to expand its global presence. \"Importantly, this is not an either/or decision between India and China,\" the spokesperson said. \"We always invest market by market based on long-term opportunities to serve athletes and grow sport around the world.\" Still, India presents an attractive opportunity for many companies looking to unlock growth abroad. Ramirez said Nike is just the latest to join a cohort of American companies looking to establish their presence in the market. While many brands began investing in China years ago, she said that trend is now pointing toward India instead, as U.S. companies see more spending pressures in the Chinese market and navigate geopolitical tensions between the two countries. \"It\'s to get in there for the long term and to make sure that they are there for when they can reap the positive effects,\" she said. \"I want to say this conversation started getting a bit more engaging in the last two to three years, when companies started investing more in India.\" India is one of the world\'s strongest market performers, driven primarily by domestic demand, investment and supply-chain diversification, according to an analysis from Accenture. With a population of over 1 billion, the country presents a growth opportunity similar to the one China offered in the early 2000s. \"It\'s going to be a fast-growing consumer base where we assume their income levels will continue to go up and they\'ll have much more purchasing power, and so maybe could we be tapping in on that market from a consumer lens,\" Chris Tomsovic, who leads Accenture\'s macro foresight team, told CNBC. Still, one of the biggest focus points for the Indian consumer is price sensitivity, which will pose a challenge for American companies trying to break into the market, he added. Even so, many domestic brands on the market are younger, meaning there could be more opportunities right now for Western companies to take market share. Another challenge is that the Chinese consumer has historically been receptive to American brands, whereas it will still take more data to understand how Indian shoppers feel about those businesses, he said. For sportswear retailers, the focus of the sports most popular in each region will also factor into that equation. \"Can they really localize the product, make the price point work? Can they adapt to the local social media technology ecosystem? Can they adapt to the local cultural values and norms that the consumer wants?\" Tomsovic said. \"If you\'re able to nail that, then I think you\'re right. Whether you\'re a footwear brand, whether you\'re an athleisure brand, I think there\'s a huge opportunity there with a billion consumers,\" he said.'),
(14, 'Business', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/14/anthropic-mythos-bank-of-america-hiring-cost.html', 'Hugh Son', 'Bank of America hired 1,000 contractors to patch software flaws exposed by Anthropic\'s Mythos', 'No headings found', 'Bank of America has hired an army of external contractors to help it patch software vulnerabilities exposed by advanced artificial intelligence models including Anthropic\'s Mythos, the bank\'s CEO, Brian Moynihan, told CNBC on Wednesday. In an interview after the bank\'s third-quarter earnings release, Moynihan said that earlier this year the company rushed to bolster a wide spectrum of its internal and vendor code using thousands of employees and outside personnel. \"That risk is real risk, and that\'s why when the frontier-type models came out. … A lot of us sat down and said, \'How do we figure out how to get through this quickly?\" Moynihan told CNBC\'s Leslie Picker. \"We put on 1,000 contractors to help us do the patching work in the second quarter, and those people remain here today, helping us, on top of the 3,000 people we have in that area,\" he said. The bank spent a \"couple hundred million plus\" on the software remediation and regulatory issues related to anti-money laundering compliance in the third quarter, Moynihan told analysts earlier Wednesday. The comments are some of the first by a major U.S. corporation to specify the manpower and dollars spent to protect software systems from Mythos, which was released to a small group of banks, technology firms and government agencies in April. They also highlight the dual-edged nature of AI: While it can help companies save money by boosting productivity, the ability of models like Mythos to uncover and exploit decades-old software flaws has also forced firms into a costly defensive scramble. \"We\'ve got to stay ahead of them, and we\'ve got to be mindful that the people doing the software development are the people trying to sell us the services,\" Moynihan said. Still, Moynihan — speaking as his company\'s shares slumped as much as 8% Wednesday — sought to reassure investors that the spending increase was temporary. \"Once you patch, you don\'t have to patch as much. … The pipeline moves through,\" Moynihan said, adding that the bank expects remediation costs to decline over time. In other places, AI is helping to reduce expenses. Bank of America has about 130 internal AI projects, and savings from those efforts are expected to rise from $300 million this year to $700 million in 2027, Moynihan told analysts. Bank of America\'s head count fell by about 3,400 employees in the past year as the firm cut roughly 5% of managers, he said. It\'s unclear how much of that workforce reduction is attributable to AI.'),
(15, 'Business', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/14/merck-moderna-cancer-vaccine-manufacturing-time.html', 'Annika Kim Constantino', 'Merck CEO tells CNBC he expects breakthrough cancer vaccine will become faster to make', 'Merck\'s new R&D facility', 'Merck CEO Rob Davis told CNBC on Wednesday that he expects the company\'s breakthrough cancer vaccine with Moderna to get faster to manufacture over time. Shortening the current six-week timeline could potentially help the companies reach more patients. \"We are going to drive for improvement, but right now the focus really is, let\'s make sure we have the supply ready, we have the market ready to bring this important innovation to the market, and then we\'ll talk about how do we accelerate,\" he told CNBC in an exclusive interview. \"But I do think over time you\'re going to see that timeline shortened.\" The personalized cancer vaccine turnaround spans from sequencing a patient\'s tumor to delivering it to the hospital. Merck and Moderna\'s vaccine is designed to target the specific mutations in each individual patient\'s tumor rather than using a one-size-fits-all approach, which makes it complicated to produce. The idea is to train the immune system to recognize and kill those unique cancer markers and then pair that response with Merck\'s blockbuster immunotherapy Keytruda, which aims to help the body attack the cancer more effectively. Davis acknowledged that manufacturing the vaccine is a \"complex system,\" but said Merck and Moderna have the process \"down to a very tight science.\" \"I\'m actually very confident that we\'re not going to have complexities in getting this to patients in a very timely fashion,\" he added. \"We\'re doing it in our clinicals, and I only see improvement going forward.\" His comments come after the two companies in August released positive initial data from a late-stage trial on the shot in combination with Keytruda in melanoma patients. Davis said the company is also examining the vaccine in trials across other tumors, including non-small cell lung cancer, types of bladder cancer and renal cell carcinoma. \"You like to talk that maybe you can start moving toward talking about a cure,\" he said. \"We\'re not there yet, but we\'re moving in a direction with technology that\'s really unprecedented.\" Davis spoke to CNBC after Merck earlier Wednesday officially opened its new 120,000-square-foot research facility, which it has named Advance, in its West Point, Pennsylvania, campus. Pennsylvania Gov. Josh Shapiro joined Merck executives and other government officials for a ribbon-cutting ceremony to celebrate the launch of the facility, which features highly automated robotic systems and advanced digital AI capabilities. Merck says the facility is designed to streamline the analysis of biological samples from the company\'s ongoing drug programs and improve its research and development. The opening of the new facility comes as Merck prepares for the upcoming loss of exclusivity of Keytruda, which is one of the world\'s top-selling drugs and is widely viewed as the backbone of modern cancer treatment. Keytruda\'s core U.S. compound patent expires in December 2028, while secondary patents extend protection into 2029 and beyond. Merck has implemented a multi-layered strategy to turn the Keytruda transition into what it views as a manageable \"hill\" rather than a sharp revenue cliff. That includes accelerating internal research and also funneling $65 billion in external acquisitions and licensing agreements over the five years to build its pipeline of next-generation medicines. The pharmaceutical giant is also developing new forms of Keytruda and combination regimens that it can transition patients onto and protect the drug\'s revenue even after key patents expire before 2030. The Food and Drug Administration in September 2025 approved the subcutaneous form of Keytruda, which is considered a more convenient version because it is administered under the skin in minutes. The standard form of the drug is an intravenous infusion that takes around 30 minutes. But Merck is facing a legal battle over the newer version in some European markets, raising questions about the market size for the drug. A Dutch court issued an injunction earlier this month blocking Merck from selling, stocking or manufacturing subcutaneous Keytruda across eight European countries: Belgium, Denmark, France, Ireland, Italy, Sweden, Switzerland and the Netherlands. That followed a patent infringement suit brought by the biotech company Halozyme Therapeutics over its own subcutaneous drug delivery platform. Merck has said it strongly disagrees with the rulings and plans to appeal. Patients can still receive the original form of Keytruda in Europe while the patent dispute unfolds. Still, Halozyme has also sued Merck for patent infringement ​in ⁠the U.S., alleging the subcutaneous formulation of Keytruda infringes 15 patents that the biotech has filed since 2011. Meanwhile, Merck is focused on rolling out a slate of new drugs. That includes Winrevair, a treatment for a rare, life-threatening lung disease that was approved in 2024 and acquired a few years earlier through its $11.5 billion acquisition of Acceleron. Another closely watched launch is Merck\'s cholesterol-lowering pill Lipfendra, which was approved by the FDA in July.'),
(16, 'Business', 'https://fortune.com/2026/10/20/elon-musk-tesla-and-spacex-weighing-merger', 'Amanda Gerut', 'Trendingnow', 'A $3.5 trillion company', 'It’s official: Tesla and SpaceX are in talks. Independent board members at the two Elon Musk-led companies have formed special committees to hash over a possible merger, Musk told investors during SpaceX’s third-quarter earnings call Tuesday. “These special committees consist of independent directors, and because I’m a conflicted party, I can’t actually be in the discussions of the special committees,” said Musk, who was asked about a potential combination during the call. “This is definitely the kind of question where I feel like I’m tap dancing through a minefield.” Tesla stock rose just over 1% in after hours trading, while SpaceX rose above 5% following the Q&A with Wall Street analysts featuring Musk, SpaceX president and COO Gwynne Shotwell, and chief financial officer Bret Johnsen. Musk said the committees haven’t reached any kind of an agreement on the terms of a potential transaction, and there’s no guarantee that they will. He noted that “some people out there assume that a SpaceX and Tesla merger is a certainty, and I think that would be overstating it.” Musk also has to walk a fine line as a conflicted party. As the CEO of both companies, he owns a roughly 20% stake in Tesla and 45% of SpaceX—and wields control over about 82% of the voting power at the latter. “It’s an odd kabuki dance where two groups of people are talking and I can’t be in the conversation,” said Musk. At the same time, Musk has made clear he believes a tie-up would be a fine idea. He cited collaboration on AI software, chip design, robotics, and connectivity, plus the planned Terafab chip plant, which is a colossal manufacturing complex being developed by SpaceX and Tesla jointly. The committees were formed “at the request of shareholders,” said Musk and on the Tesla side it would need support from a majority of all outstanding shares, which Musk described as “quite a high hurdle.” Tesla filed its annual shareholder proxy report on Monday, and it doesn’t include a merger proposal for shareholders to vote on. Rumors of a tie-up, however, have swirled for months, even before SpaceX’s blockbuster IPO in June. At Tuesday’s closing prices, a combined Tesla-SpaceX would have a total market value of about $3.5 trillion, with SpaceX at about $2 trillion and Tesla at nearly $1.5 trillion. A formal deal price wouldn’t come until much further down the road, if at all. Musk would own about a third of the combined entity. The companies are already financially entangled, including a $2 billion investment by Tesla in SpaceX, Tesla’s Monday proxy statement confirmed. There are also multiple areas of overlap between the Tesla and SpaceX boards. Ira Ehrenpreis, founder of venture capital firm DBL Partners, chairs SpaceX’s compensation and nominating committee, which is a single committee at SpaceX, as well as the two separate committees at Tesla. And Tesla board member James Murdoch, who has been a director at the electric-vehicle maker since 2017, testified in a previous legal challenge over Musk’s pay package at Tesla that he had previously invested in SpaceX. The disclosure from Musk came as SpaceX reported its first operating profit since its June IPO. Revenues for the third quarter fiscal 2027 year rose 161% to $14.58 billion, well above the $12.86 billion analysts expected, according to Visible Alpha consensus from S&P Global Market Intelligence. Operating revenue clocked in at $2.79 billion, apnd net income was $4.4 billion. Revenue from SpaceX’s AI business, which was renamed “Super Intelligence,” nearly tripled from the second quarter to $7.56 billion, surpassing Starlink’s $5.25 billion for the first time since the company went public. In his usual fashion, Musk offered up aggressively optimistic thoughts about the future of SpaceX, claiming SpaceX’s breakneck buildout pace implies annual recurring revenue of $500 billion to $1 trillion by the end of 2027. Meanwhile, Johnsen reaffirmed a target of $100 billion in annualized revenue by the end of this year. At its third-quarter pace, SpaceX is bringing in about $58 billion a year. Hitting the $100 billion figure would take about $8.3 billion of revenue in December, roughly 70% more than its average month last quarter. Musk’s range would require about $42 billion to $83 billion a month by December 2027. Tesla did not immediately respond to a request for comment.'),
(17, 'Business', 'https://fortune.com/2026/10/19/attorney-general-should-sue-congress-article-v-national-debt-fiscal-responsibility', 'Steve H. Hanke', 'Trendingnow', 'No headings found', 'Now that Constitution Day has passed, it is time to face reality. Only a fiscal responsibility constitutional amendment can force Congress to restore and sustain fiscal sanity. To light the fuse, it is time for a state attorney general to stand up and assert the rights of the states under Article V of the U.S. Constitution and hold Congress accountable for its failure to act. State attorneys general clearly have standing to sue and should do so. Article V of the Constitution states that Congress “shall call” a convention when two-thirds of the states (34) file applications. As Alexander Hamilton emphatically declared in Federalist No. 85: “The words of this article are peremptory. The Congress ‘shall call a convention.’ Nothing in this particular is left to the discretion of that body.” Hamilton went to great lengths to make his point so that a compromise that protected states’ rights could be made to facilitate the ratification of the initial Constitution. Over three years ago, the Federal Fiscal Sustainability Foundation (FFSF) determined that, as far back as 1979, a total of 39 states had active applications for an Article V amendments convention. Of the 39 states with active applications, 30 were limited to fiscal responsibility and nine were plenary applications. Total applications rose to 40 states in 1983, of which 32 were fiscal responsibility only applications. In addition, as recently as 2017, more than the required two-thirds of the states had active applications, but Congress failed to act. Importantly, rescinded applications are not included in these counts. These facts have been confirmed by the National Federalism Commission, an official interstate governmental body which is preparing for an Article V amendments convention. Shockingly, it was not until 2015 that Congress assigned responsibility to the House Judiciary Committee for receiving, storing, making public, and counting state Article V applications. Even more shocking, the House Judiciary Committee has failed to effectively discharge its responsibilities. As it turns out, when it comes to calling an Article V convention, the nation’s founders recognized that Congress has a clear conflict of interest. An Article V convention is only necessary when Congress fails to propose and pass amendments on matters that require action. These include such things as restoring fiscal sanity, campaign finance reform, and term limits. Given its conflict, Congress should assign the responsibility for receiving, storing, and counting state Article V applications to the Comptroller General of the United States, who heads the U.S. Government Accountability Office (GAO). While there are clearly valid reasons to call a state-led Article V amendments convention to propose a fiscal responsibility amendment, there are groups who oppose any Article V convention. They include groups on the far right, such as the John Birch Society, and the far left, such as Common Cause. Both groups argue that an Article V convention would be a “runaway” convention, one that would invite a total revision of the U.S. Constitution. They are wrong. Once the convention convenes, the majority of states will decide on the scope of the convention. Today, a majority of the states have active applications that are limited to fiscal responsibility only. The majority will ensure that the Article V convention will be limited to fiscal responsibility only. Some argue that the two-thirds (34-state) requirement has not been met because plenary applications should not count. Constitutional scholars on both the right and the left have agreed that such plenary (any subject) applications should be counted. In fact, when New Jersey rescinded its plenary application in 2021, it noted that one of the reasons it did so was because it would count in achieving the 34-state requirement. Others argue that the courts will not consider a suit against Congress for its dereliction of duty, asserting that it is a political question or that it involves the Speech or Debate Clause. Such is not the case. Under Article V, calling a convention is an express, ministerial, and non-discretionary obligation of Congress. In addition, a suit would not be against any member of Congress, and the call does not involve speech or debate. Some also assert that the filings for a convention are too late. But they are incorrect. None of the applicable state applications carry expiration dates. In addition, rescinded applications are not counted. However, once the thirty-four-state threshold is crossed, the obligation to call an Article V convention is immediately triggered. Congress must not be rewarded for its failure to act. Importantly, the President and the executive branch would not be parties to a legal suit by a state’s attorney general. Indeed, a suit would focus on the failure of Congress to discharge its express, enumerated, non-discretionary, and ministerial responsibility under Article V of the Constitution. It is time to hold Congress accountable for its failure to discharge its express constitutional duty. It has been derelict for far too long. A suit by the states is the only realistic remedy. Now is the time to act. Steve H. Hanke is a senior contributing columnist at Fortune, a professor of applied economics at Johns Hopkins University, and a director of the Federal Fiscal Sustainability Foundation, which advocates for a fiscal responsibility amendment and a statutory fiscal commission. He is also co-editor, with Barry W. Poulson and John Merrifield, of Public Debt Sustainability: International Perspectives (Lexington Books, 2022). David M. Walker is the former Comptroller General of the United States and the chairman of the Federal Fiscal Sustainability Foundation. He is co-author, with Joe Penland, Sr., of the forthcoming Saving Social Security and America’s Future: Common Sense Solutions. The opinions expressed in Fortune.com commentary pieces are solely the views of their authors and do not necessarily reflect the opinions and beliefs of Fortune.');
INSERT INTO `articlescrapper` (`ID`, `Topic`, `SiteURL`, `Author`, `Title`, `Header`, `Text`) VALUES
(18, 'Business', 'https://fortune.com/2026/10/20/lakers-clippers-manchester-city-scandals-owners', 'Joshua Hong', 'Trendingnow', 'Getting around the cap | Manufacturing revenue | When the money is retirees’ | Why it’s so hard to stop', 'It takes a fortune far bigger than any sports team to buy one—and that fortune rarely sits still. It’s invested in startups, private holding companies and insurers. This year, three of the richest owners in sports have come under scrutiny for deals in which they had money on both sides. Steve Ballmer had invested in Aspiration, the financial services company that signed his star player, Kawhi Leonard, to an endorsement deal. Manchester City’s owner, through his investment vehicle, covered some of the fees the club’s sponsors were supposed to pay, an independent commission found. And insurers controlled by former Los Angeles Lakers owner Mark Walter lent billions to companies tied to his financial empire, leading to lawsuits and an SEC investigation. The consequences are piling up. The NBA fined the Clippers $30 million, a ruling the team is contesting, while ordering Ballmer to have zero contact with the team for a year. An independent Premier League commission found City guilty of financial charges, findings the club is appealing, with the much-rumored sanctions still to come. Walter sold the Lakers after owning them barely more than a year, and federal prosecutors are examining Walter’s companies; he denies any wrongdoing. Related-party transactions, or business arrangements between entities that are connected through common ownership, are at the center of the biggest sports scandals this year. These transactions aren’t inherently illegal—companies routinely do business with affiliates, and sports owners can legitimately hold investments across multiple industries. After all, how else would they afford a team in the first place? But the risk surfaces when those same relationships create conflicts of interest that obscure the true flow of money or help owners get around rules designed to protect investors, policyholders and the integrity of the sport. “Certainly the possibility is inevitable,” Rachael Jones, a principal at law firm McKool Smith and former state and federal prosecutor, told Fortune. Whether those conflicts will result in a violation, she said, depends on the rules governing the transaction. Part of why more of these cases may be surfacing now, she suggested, is that they’re easier to find: Filings that once required a trip to the library are increasingly online. Sports are not unique in attracting potentially abusive arrangements, according to Jones. But as the industry grows more lucrative and the owners expand into additional businesses, the opportunities for exploitation grow. “Sports is a big business,” Jones said. “It’s profit, so it just can attract misuse.” Who stands to lose depends on which rules are at stake. In the NBA, it’s rival teams that play under the same salary cap. In the Premier League, it’s rival clubs held to the same spending rules. In Walter’s case, it’s policyholders, who have sued, alleging that his insurers misrepresented their investments. Last month, NBA owner Steve Ballmer and the Clippers were hit with major penalties, including a $30 million fine, following an investigation into endorsement arrangements involving two-time NBA Finals MVP Kawhi Leonard. The league found that the Clippers and Ballmer facilitated off-court income opportunities for Leonard, which included arrangements with the now-bankrupt financial services company Aspiration. Ballmer had invested $50 million in Aspiration beforehand—and $10 million after Leonard’s arrival in LA. That put Ballmer’s money on both sides: He owned the team paying Leonard’s salary and was an investor in the company that signed Leonard to an endorsement deal. “The NBA’s collectively bargained system for determining player compensation is a fundamental component of the basketball competition that the league oversees for the benefit of the teams and players and ultimately the fans,” NBA Commissioner Adam Silver said in a press release. “I am deeply disappointed by the flagrant violations of our rules and by the Clippers’ institutional and leadership failures that led to this misconduct. The severity of the penalties reflects the seriousness of the violations.” Ballmer is by far the richest owner in the NBA—with a net worth of $156 billion—and the Clippers are now worth roughly $7.5 billion, nearly four times what Ballmer had bought the team for at $2 billion, according to Forbes. And Ballmer and the Clippers remain adamant the penalties—and the allegations of misconduct—are incorrect. “We are exploring every legal remedy to address this gross injustice,” a letter from the Clippers sent to Silver shared by reporter Marc Stein on X read. Ballmer and the Clippers did not respond to requests for comment from Fortune. In September, an independent Premier League commission found that Manchester City arranged “sham” commercial agreements that artificially inflated revenue. The club also filed misstated accounts and concealed its finances, the commission found. The club is appealing the decision. According to the investigation, Manchester City arranged commercial deals with a number of its sponsors which were only required to pay a portion of the relevant sponsorship fees. The remainder was funded by Abu Dhabi United Group Investment & Development Ltd, which owned the club—making the owner’s money sit on both sides of the sponsorships. The football club was found guilty of 114 out of 115 financial charges from the Premier League from an investigation on a period between the 2009-10 and 2017-18 seasons. Manchester City artificially inflated revenue and understated expenses by roughly the equivalent of $1 billion, according to the Premier League’s statement following the investigation’s findings. The charges concerned the league’s financial rules at the time. Since 2021, the Premier League has also had Associated Party Transaction (APT) rules, aimed at commercial deals between clubs and entities tied to their owners. “It is a key responsibility of the Premier League to ensure that the Rules, approved by the clubs themselves, are upheld to protect the integrity of the competition,” Richard Masters, chief executive of the Premier League, said in a press release. “It is paramount that the league remains competitive and fair for all clubs and for the fans.” Manchester City is owned by City Football Group, which is majority-owned by Sheikh Mansour bin Zayed Al Nahyan, a member of Abu Dhabi’s royal family. Manchester City did not respond to a request for comment from Fortune. Mark Walter, CEO of Guggenheim Partners and former majority owner of the Los Angeles Lakers, has financial holdings across separate firms that have become the focal point for interconnected businesses in sports—and the subject of a federal investigation. In July 2026, disclosures about affiliated investments at insurers linked to Walter drew scrutiny to how they were tied to his sports ownership. Federal prosecutors are examining whether companies tied to his financial empire improperly handled billions of dollars in loans from insurance companies that he separately controls. Amid the ongoing investigation, the Los Angeles Lakers were then sold in August by Walter to new owners Josh Kushner and Bob Iger in a $12.5 billion deal—making Walter’s majority ownership of the team last less than a year when he bought it from the Buss family in June 2025. However, Walter and TWG Global—Walter’s holding company—posit the move was unrelated to the investigation completely. “Regarding the proposed sale of the Los Angeles Lakers for $12.5 billion, Mr. Walter was approached by Josh Kushner and his team about this transaction and the agreement represents a 25% premium to the price paid by Mr. Walter less than a year ago,” a TWG press release read. Walter and his company were also clear that his other sports assets—such as the Los Angeles Dodgers—were not for “fire sale” as well. “Regarding the Los Angeles Dodgers, to be clear, the team is not being sold and no sale process has been initiated,” the statement continued. Two insurers in Walter’s orbit, Delaware Life Insurance Co. and Clear Spring Life and Annuity Co., conducted internal reviews after receiving federal grand-jury subpoenas and significantly restated what they described as errors in prior financial reporting. Policyholders and retirees have since filed lawsuits alleging that the insurers misrepresented their investments. But Guggenheim and Walter deny any wrongdoing or fraud. “As it relates to Guggenheim, while the investigations originated with a whistleblower concern, TWG and Guggenheim have demonstrated there was no wrongdoing,” a press release from TWG read. Guggenheim Partners and Mark Walter did not respond to requests for comment from Fortune. Jones said the potential consequences for policyholders could be severe if an insurer’s financial position deteriorated enough to stop it from meeting its obligations. Someone who had purchased an annuity expecting retirement income, for example, could face uncertainty if the insurer can’t pay the claim, she said. But Jones thinks we haven’t seen the end of it yet. “Certainly, with the more somebody controls, the more power they have, and things could be manipulated,” Jones explained. “I think that there’s always, to some degree, related party transactions. Sometimes they’re not illegal—often, it’s that it has to be disclosed so that somebody goes in with their eyes wide open and understands the risks.” “What I notice is with Abu Dhabi and Steve Ballmer, it’s like we’re trying to circumvent the rules that are there,” she continued. “So what’ll be interesting to me is to see if the sports leagues will have more rules that come into play in the future.” Sports leagues have all employed certain financial rules to prevent misconduct. But those rules govern the team’s side of a deal. The owner’s other businesses—a fintech startup, a private investment group, a life insurer—answer to other regulators, or to none. In the U.S., insurers are overseen by state insurance departments, which have no role in policing sports. Private ownership can also limit how much outsiders know about a team’s finances. It can make it more difficult to determine whether a sponsorship reflects genuine commercial value, whether a loan is being made on market terms or whether money is moving between related entities in ways that benefit one party at another’s expense. “Somebody said to me one time, you know, we haven’t created a government program yet that hasn’t found somebody that’s going to try and perhaps successfully defraud it, and I think that’s true in any industry,” Jones said.'),
(19, 'Business', 'https://fortune.com/2026/10/20/as-billionaires-fight-tax-hike-jensen-huang-paying-8-billion-is-privilidge-just-afraid-of-being-poor', 'Emma Burleigh', 'Trendingnow', 'Huang urges people, ‘Move to California, don’t leave’ as billionaires flee', 'California is home to dozens of entrepreneurs who have made billions off their companies. Now some are packing up and leaving as the coastal state weighs tax hikes against the rich, but Nvidia CEO and longtime California resident Jensen Huang is happy to pay his fair share. “I don’t mind paying taxes. The more you make, the more taxes you pay. I’m okay with that,” Huang said in a recent interview with CBS Sunday Morning. “I am the ultimate American Dream.” Billionaires like Google’s cofounder Sergey Brin and former CEO Eric Schmidt have been waging war against Proposition 40 in California: a one-time 5% tax on residents with assets over $1 billion. The state hopes this will increase temporary revenue by tens of billions spread over several years to fund health care and education. And some of California’s ultrarich are fighting back. Tech billionaires have raised more than $340 million and spend more than $1 million daily in a campaign to defeat Prop 40, according to the Guardian. Others like Palantir’s Peter Thiel and Meta’s Mark Zuckerberg have already left the state for more tax-friendly areas. But Huang, the world’s ninth-richest person with an eye-watering $180 billion net worth, isn’t afraid to pay up. The leader of the world’s largest chip company reasoned that his success wouldn’t have been possible without the opportunities America afforded him. He was able to pursue a high-level education, create a new company, pursue his dreams, and meet the people that “made Nvidia possible.” Huang has lived a success story that millions dream of—and he’s happy to contribute to the same system that nurtured his ambitions. “The fact that I can afford to pay $8 billion in taxes over five years is a privilege,” Huang continued. “I’m not afraid of paying taxes. I’m just afraid of being poor.” Fortune reached out to Nvidia for comment. The “Make Billionaires Pay Their Fair Share Act” was introduced in March by Rep. Ro Khanna (D-Calif.) and Sen. Bernie Sanders (D-Vt.). Ahead of Prop 40’s Jan. 1, 2026, residency cutoff, a slew of billionaires left the state. Google cofounders Larry Page and Brin moved their business entities to Florida and Nevada, and Travis Kalanick, the former CEO of Uber, also changed addresses to Austin. As some billionaires balk at the tax hike, Huang has said that “I haven’t thought about it even once.” Instead, he’s continued to be outspoken about the upsides of living in the Golden State—and called on others to make California their permanent home. “I say to everybody, ‘Move to California, don’t leave.’ It’s the highest taxes in the world, but it’s okay,” Huang said at the Stanford Graduate School of Business earlier this year. Aside from having great weather, California is also a hotbed for innovation, Huang has reasoned. Nvidia’s office is located in Santa Clara, right in the heart of Silicon Valley where “the talent pool is,” Huang has said. He argues that it’s a choice to set up shop in the tech hub—and whatever taxes come with operating there, “so be it.” However, Huang isn’t sold on being taxed for everything. While he has no issue with Prop 40, he is skeptical that company robots and AI tokens should be taxed. Microsoft cofounder Bill Gates championed the idea; the tech mogul argued that by not taxing those tools, but still forking over payroll taxes on employee earnings, employers are incentivized to replace humans with machines. In response, Huang said his “remedies will be a little different.” “I’m in favor of taxes,” Huang said in an interview with Fox Business’s The Claman Countdown earlier this year. “And I think that…for anybody who is productive, it’s a great way for us to contribute back to society and the economy. But the fact of the matter is, there are probably lots of different ways to approach this.”'),
(20, 'Business', 'https://fortune.com/2026/09/26/how-social-media-is-changing-the-rare-watch-market-instagram-tiktok-watch-collecting', 'Adam Erace', 'Trendingnow', 'Wrist Candy | 1942 Patek Philippe 570 | 1976 Rolex Cellini Wood Dial 4122 | 2017 Akrivia Regulator Tourbillon AK04 | 2022 Naoya Hida & Co. Type 1D', 'A Brazilian aviator and a French jeweler walk into a bar. The year: 1900. The place: Maxim’s, the Paris clubhouse for the Belle Époque intelligentsia to discuss matters such as, How much absinthe is too much absinthe? On this day, the question was more practical: How does a pilot tell time on his pocket watch without crashing his airship? Alberto Santos-Dumont was the son of a French-Brazilian coffee tycoon, and a dashing explorer and aeronaut. His pal, Louis Cartier—another nepo baby as the scion of the Cartier jewelry empire, and a brilliant designer in his own right—proposed a solution: What if a watch could be fastened to his ami’s arm, instead of kept in the pocket of his trousers? This is how the modern men’s wristwatch was born. Cartier debuted the delicate, square-faced Santos in 1911 and has been producing versions ever since. They range from svelte relics to a showy platinum-cased 90th anniversary edition with a salmon dial and indigo Breguet hands that fetched nearly $48,000 at Christie’s last year. “Collectibility-wise, Cartier is probably one of the biggest brands that exists,” says Tom Collins, owner of vintage watch dealer the Wrist Watcher Ltd., “but there are so, so many variants, it’s hard to pinpoint which is a collector’s piece.” There are clues horologists look for, like the number of grams of gold engraved on the back of the two-tone Santos Carrée, a piece Collins recently acquired for his tiny counter in London’s tony Mayfair. Debuted in 1978, this particular model represents a significant inflection point in design and mechanics for Cartier. The house recast the Santos in a chunky, sportier silhouette; squared the face (carrée is French for “square”); exposed its screws; and integrated a bracelet in stainless steel and 18-karat gold. It also went automatic. The Carrée is my favorite Santos, a cuspy chameleon that, depending on styling, can give off last days of disco or Bret Easton Ellis protagonist. When I saw it, I had to have it. Where I saw it, though, was not at Grays Antique Market, the agora where the Wrist Watcher Ltd. rents space, but while scrolling on my phone during a red-eye from Addis Ababa to Heathrow last spring. On random evenings at 8 p.m. U.K. time on @TheWristWatcher1, 22-year-old Collins drops a cache of treasures to his 260,000 watch-fanatic followers, of which I am one. “Presenting the cleanest Cartier Santos Carrée on the market!” the caption read. I immediately slotted a visit into my London plans so I could take my precious home. Adam Golden of Menta Watches, a high-end dealer in downtown Miami, calls what I experienced seeing that watch pop up on my feed an I want that moment. When he started his timepiece journey in 2012, these lightning strikes largely happened IRL. Today, he tells me, “you essentially can see every watch that was ever produced just by searching on social media.” From a nostalgic birth-year Casio to a “Grand Seiko that only three people in the world have ever heard of,” an heirloom that was passed down through generations can be yours in just a few clicks. Luxury watch brands have long looked down their noses at e-commerce, leaving the secondary market to gladly take the crumbs and make them into cake. The reseller market hit $10.5 billion in value in the first half of 2026, a 37.2% increase year over year, according to research platform EveryWatch. Even for dealers like Collins and Golden who maintain physical spaces, social media is a growing gateway for buyers under 50. Over the past decade, a few brands have noticed the trend and jumped into social media. One pivotal moment was Omega’s “Speedy Tuesday” watch—a limited edition produced in 2017 as a tribute to online fans of Omega’s Speedmaster line. A community spun off from a weekly column in the digital magazine Fratello Watches had created a weekly social media ritual around the #SpeedyTuesday hashtag. “For those that don’t get or believe that social media can have real impact,” says Paul Croughton, an impeccably dressed London editor-in-chief and watch enthusiast, “that was one of the most prestigious watch brands on the planet being directly influenced by a bunch of watch nerds.” Social media has since melted the line between dealer and influencer, collector and creator, and there’s a flavor of watch content for every consumer. My Instagram Discover page is a narcotic carousel of supple leather and gleaming metal. On the rare occasions that I pull up TikTok, the grid of faces on #WatchTok assaults my geriatric millennial senses: diamond dealers doing on-the-(47th)-street interviews; authenticators revealing results with the breathlessness of a Maury paternity drama; influencers unboxing Tiffany-blue Daytonas and Seamaster Aqua Terras with gloved fingers. A slightly manic note can creep into this corner of social media. The mostly male, mostly young consumers of this content came up in the pandemic days of meme stock windfalls, speculative crypto investments, and hypebeast product drops. This is how you get imbroglios like last spring’s Audemars Piguet x Swatch Royal Pop launch, which generated headlines like “Global chaos triggered by a limited-edition watch collaboration” (ABC World  News Tonight) and “Swatch psychos stampede Long Island mall for special AP release as cop pepper-sprays mob”—a doozy, even by New York Post standards. For this mob, watches are as much assets to flip as trophies to collect. In a K-shaped economy with an evaporating entry-level white-collar job market, can you blame them? An heirloom that was passed down through generations can be yours in just a few clicks. Golden doesn’t. He recognizes that social media is probably the path to building a community of watch lovers. “For this industry to thrive and exist in the next 10, 20, 30 years, there needs to be a younger generation,” he says. “I’m reminded of my dad, who’s a stamp collector. There’s no younger generation getting into stamps. That’s going to die out.” (#StampTok would like a word.) And while the platform and culture may have changed, the thrill of the hunt—and occasional buyer’s remorse—has not. “Sometimes the chase is more thrilling than that moment of ownership,” Croughton says. “You put it on the wrist, and you’re kinda like, ‘I like it … But it doesn’t do what I thought it would do. It doesn’t change me as a spiritual being.’ ” We do put an awful lot of pressure on luxury goods to transmogrify our mortal bodies. Whether it’s a rare Vacheron, Vuitton luggage, or a Zegna suit, the costumes we put on contain their own stories, which in turn tell stories about us. Setting out on my quest for the Carrée I’d seen on Instagram, I wondered, What story will this watch tell about me? “Here is man of the world,” perhaps. “Here is a man with recommendations for cocktails in Paris and tailors in Bangkok.” Here is a man who does not know how to Google a shop’s operating hours, apparently. I walked into Grays Antique Market and found the Wrist Watcher counter dark. Not that it would have mattered. With his social media watch drops, Collins says, “generally, 80% to 90% will go within the first 30 minutes. Some even go within 60 seconds.” The Carrée I’d fallen for, priced at around $5,000, was likely long gone. Then came a stroke of serendipity that no social media algorithm could replicate. Wandering past cases of Russian silver and Wedgwood china, I happened upon the Watch Locator, a storefront on the lower level. Owner Rahul Shamshad, a dyed-in-the-wool salesman who talks a mile a minute, keeps a shop that feels more like a professor’s cluttered office than the antiseptic or manospheric galleries that typify the genre. He’s on Instagram too, the 38-year-old told me, but “to be honest, I tend to do a lot of walk-in.” The experience had a warm, old-school glow, not unlike the mid-century Pateks that are Shamshad’s specialty. Those were out of my budget, so I lingered on his deep bench of Omega De Villes, originally a Speedmaster subset whose popularity earned the design its own collection. The unusual hexagonal dial of the 1970–71 De Ville Reference 111.0103 got me. I watched its hands, as thin as eyelashes, ticking across a linen-finish champagne face and thought, I want that. In my case, the decisive click turned out to be the clasp closing around my wrist. This article appears in the October/November 2026 issue of  Fortune with the headline “The algorithm of desire: How social media remade the rare watch market.” Four watches with Instagram main-character energy A big boy from a time, says Rahul Shamshad, “when men were wearing much smaller watches,” this antique Patek stands out for its presence on the wrist and its rose-gold dial. “Only a handful of this configuration ever entered the market.” A Patek devotee paid $54,500 to add it to his collection. “Modern Rolex is so conservative, but Rolex before the 1990s was a brand that experimented and innovated a huge amount,” says Tom Collins. One example he “really regrets selling” for about $7,400 last year is this handsome Cellini with a hexagonal burled-wood face. “This was the fourth model produced by Rexhep Rexhepi, the genius visionary behind the Akrivia brand,” says Adam Golden. “It is estimated that less than 10 of these were produced in total.” Golden won’t disclose the sale price, but puts its current market value between $3 million and $5 million. A handsomely proportioned modern watch in disguise, this edition from independent Japanese watchmaker Naoya Hida features hand-carved numerals and an interesting inset seconds track. “It’s very subtle and elegant,” says Paul Croughton, who paid around $14,000 for it. “You wouldn’t really know what it was unless you’re particularly into watches.”'),
(21, 'Business', 'https://www.theguardian.com/uni-where-change-happens/2026/sep/03/how-the-university-of-essex-prepares-students-for-ethical-leadership', 'Christian Koch', 'Beyond the balance sheet: how the University of Essex prepares its students for ethical leadership', 'No headings found', 'A marketing management student on how Essex Business School is equipping the leaders of tomorrow with the skills they need As a student on the cusp of starting her career, Amy Sutton has a few non-negotiables about where she wants to work. Like many young people, working for a company that pollutes the environment, mistreats staff or churns out unhealthy products simply isn’t an option. “One of the first things people look at when applying for jobs is the values a business holds – you want to work for a company whose ethics line up with your own,” says the 20-year-old University of Essex student, who is about to start the third year of her marketing management degree. “Open-minded and inclusive businesses have a massive impact [with young job-seekers].” As Sutton suggests, businesses will need to meet higher standards if they want to recruit and retain the talent they need. Although today many companies are falling over themselves to broadcast their “purpose”, it means little if the person at the top isn’t driven by the same desire for change. Being a leader in 2026 is unlike anything that came before. The days when a CEO would make employees dread Monday mornings, or only step into the spotlight for the annual AGM, are long gone. Today’s leaders still need to deliver commercial success, true, but also need to look beyond the balance sheet, walking a tightrope between profits, climate commitments, AI ethics and supporting employees’ mental health. The University of Essex is preparing students for that future. Ethical leadership is woven into the teaching at its business school, from the new corporate sustainability MSc to modules covering social entrepreneurship, good governance and green finance. Having studied at the UK’s 12th best university in the Guardian University Guide 2026, students step into their careers as purpose-led leaders, with the business nous needed to navigate an increasingly uncertain world. Doing the right thing needn’t be bad for profits either. The UK’s ethical shopping market is worth about £141bn annually, while 73% of global consumers actively avoid businesses that don’t show empathy, according to a Zurich Insurance Group study. As a marketing student, Sutton realises the brand value of being a responsible business: after all, a social media shaming can quickly turn companies into pariahs. “Every promotional campaign needs to be ethical and maintain a standard you want the business to represent,” she says. It’s important for Sutton to study at a university that practises what it preaches when it comes to sustainability. Essex Business School was the UK’s first carbon-zero business school building when it opened in 2015, saving more than a tonne of carbon a day thanks to its efficient timber design and solar panels. “It’s reassuring knowing I’m studying in a building which isn’t harming the environment,” she says. Sutton also likes working in its winter garden, designed to support student wellbeing with its own microclimate, rainwater-harvesting pond and tropical foliage, all under an Eden Project-like domed roof. At Essex Business School, responsible leadership is felt in the classroom too. “The lecturers here make students feel comfortable – I always feel I can put my hand up in class or say I don’t understand something,” says Sutton. “It’s really helped me come out of my shell. I’m now a student ambassador who gives campus tours and fronts LinkedIn videos about the business school. I couldn’t have done that two years ago.” For entrepreneur Andew Hulbert, an Essex Business School graduate, that sense of confidence and belonging has had a lasting impact. He went on to found the facilities management service provider Pareto FM in 2014, a company that prides itself on its inclusion policies in the workplace and its ESG initiatives. He recently returned to Essex to deliver a workshop on leadership in the modern day. “What I always loved about the university was its culture of openness and complete acceptance of freedom of expression,” he says. “Students could come to Essex and truly be who they wanted to be … Coupled with the huge international student presence on site, you couldn’t help but experience new things and become a better, more well-rounded person.” This culture of openness chimes with the idea of “psychological safety” – the phrase describing an environment where employees can ask questions and admit mistakes without fear of embarrassment. A study by Google’s Project Aristotle found that psychological safety was the biggest factor in team success rather than having a superstar entrepreneur at the helm. “Psychological safety is perhaps the single most important factor for employee wellbeing today,” says Hulbert. “As new generations enter the workforce it is of paramount importance that they can represent themselves as they wish in their chosen workplace. For many generations they were grateful to have a job; now employers have to work just as hard to attract talent. It’s a genuine two-way street. Essex taught me the power of tolerance and acceptance, and the power of bringing people together and allowing them to be themselves.” Alongside the rise of the “compassionate CEO” (emerging during the pandemic’s daily video check-ins), there’s been a growing trend for leaders to become “corporate activists”, taking a stance on social issues such as environmental pledges or racial inequality. The University of Essex has history here. Since being founded in 1964, it has championed social justice and diversity, whether it’s student protests during the Vietnam war, social progressiveness (it established a day nursery in the 1970s) or that sustainable campus. Its willingness to challenge the status quo has created real-world change through alumni including Nobel peace prize-winning former Costa Rican president Óscar Arias, architect Daniel Libeskind, or historian Anton Jäger, currently turning heads among political cognoscenti with his book, Hyperpolitics. The international outlook at the university and Essex Business School – students from more than 140 countries learn there – also equips graduates with the global perspective needed to thrive in an increasingly multipolar business world. The university is also known for social mobility and inclusion. Nearly half of students come from families without a history of higher education, while 18% have a disability. Sutton is already thinking about how responsible business will shape her own career – her dream job is to work in Formula One. With her sights set on a career in social media marketing, she’s well aware of the ethical considerations such a role entails. “[Marketers] have a responsibility to ensure social media is used responsibly,” she says. “Keeping users’ data secure is also important, as is content: information needs to come from credible sources.” As she considers her career ahead, Sutton believes responsible leadership starts with something simple: creating an environment where people feel valued. “If you want to run a team, you need to make people feel confident they can talk to you when they don’t understand things,” she says. “The University of Essex 100% makes me feel that way.” Find out more about studying for a marketing or business degree at Essex Business School'),
(22, 'Business', 'https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/20/countries-pushing-vital-global-climate-report-delayed', 'Fiona Harvey', 'Why have countries been pushing for a vital global climate report to be delayed?', 'What has happened? | Who wants a delay and why? | Would a delay enable more science from developing countries? | What do civil society activists think? | What happens now?', 'Saudi Arabia, Russia and China among those claiming developed countries have skewed findings of IPCC review The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change suspended an important meeting on Friday in Addis Ababa without setting out a firm timetable for its next report. The world’s governments are due to meet in less than a month for the Cop31 climate summit, and this leaves the future of scientific advice to governments on the climate in doubt. The IPCC – the UN-convened body of the world’s leading climate scientists – met for a week but, despite progress in many areas, was unable to come to agreement on the substantive issue of setting a launch date for its next comprehensive assessment of global knowledge of climate science. The seventh assessment report, known as AR7, was expected to be published in 2028, seven years after the last report in 2021. But some want this timetable to be delayed to 2029, or later. A loose group of governments led by Saudi Arabia, Russia and India, and also including China and Kenya, have raised objections to publishing the report in 2028, arguing that climate science is dominated by developed countries, which they say has skewed its findings. But many scientists, and experts, worry that a delay would prevent AR7 being used to inform the next round of pledges of emissions cuts, due to be discussed by governments at the Cop33 climate summit in late 2028. Under the timetable set out by the 2015 Paris agreement, 2028 is a crucial year as it will be the second “global stocktake”, by which governments’ pledges on emissions are scrutinised to ensure they are in line with the goal of holding temperature rises to 1.5C above preindustrial levels. Saudi Arabia has a long history of obstructing climate action, including seeking to water down previous IPCC reports, and has employees of its oil company and scientists with oil links on the IPCC committees. Most of the science that will be in AR7 has already been published and is now under review. IPCC reports take years to compile, combing nearly every possible source of data, and that is done at an early point in its reporting cycle. Establishing new sources at this late point is more difficult. The IPCC argues that it already takes pains to include scientists from around the world and keeps a critical eye on equity to ensure against developed country bias. Much of the basic science it relies on is based on the global workings of the climate system, and understanding the underlying physics and chemistry, which do not differ from developed to developing countries. Critics also point out that Saudi Arabia, Russia, China and India are all powerful economies with well-established scientific institutions and research bases – if they wanted to produce scientific models themselves, they could have done so by now, or could have provided financial assistance to other developing countries to do so. One possible compromise is for the IPCC to start working now on new ways to bring in more science from the developing world, and undertake a critique of its processes to that end, while keeping to the 2028 timetable for the first part of its four-part report, which deals with the underlying physical science, and allowing the other parts – which cover the impacts of climate breakdown around the world, and solutions to it – to be delayed. They are divided. Some are against a delay and view it as a danger to progress on cutting emissions. Some leading figures support it, arguing that whose research informs the science, and whether scientists from developing countries can participate meaningfully, is as important as speed. Others have issued statements deploring the row while carefully not taking sides. Mohamed Adow, director of the Power Shift Africa thinktank, said: “It is deeply frustrating that governments have failed to agree a way forward. Developing countries must have a meaningful voice in shaping that science, and wealthy nations must provide the funding needed to make that possible. But legitimate concerns about fairness cannot become an excuse for endless delays.” Oscar Soria, director at the Common Initiative group, said: “The failure to agree the timeline is not a procedural detail, it has real consequences.. The answer is not to choose between urgency and equity, but to provide the resources and support needed to achieve both.” A group of 117 scientists, many of them from developing countries, including several from China and one from India, have written an open letter vowing to push ahead with their work. “Our message to governments and to the world is simple: we will keep working,” they wrote. “Equity is built into how our assessment is being drafted. Author teams are working from a far wider evidence base than in AR6. It includes literature from the global south, non-English sources, grey literature and Indigenous knowledge and local knowledge.” The IPCC’s chair, Jim Skea, has also pledged to reconvene as soon as possible. The issue is certain to come up at the Cop31 talks in Turkey next month, where governments will be asked what they are doing to show a sense of urgency in tackling the climate crisis, in the face of what is likely to be the strongest El Niño in decades, supercharged by global heating, and likely to cause huge amounts of damage – in developed and developing countries.'),
(23, 'Business', 'https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/20/revolut-down-app-cards-service-disruption', 'Kalyeena Makortoff', 'Google cloud failure hits millions as UK courts and banks knocked offline', 'No headings found', 'Services restored for most users, but power fault stalled hearings and left Revolut, Lloyds and HSBC customers unable to pay or move money An outage at Google’s cloud services in the UK has caused widespread disruption, hitting courts and leaving millions of customers unable to access or send cash at banks including Lloyds, HSBC and Revolut. Google blamed a “power event” for the failure at its London-based Cloud computing operations on Tuesday, which affected its “europe-west2-b zone”. The US tech company said services were starting to come back online as of 1pm London time, and were back online for most customers by 3pm, but not before critical financial transactions and legal proceedings were disrupted. That included the video system used by defendants, lawyers and other parties to attend court hearings remotely across England and Wales, which failed to work throughout the morning. The issue was believed to have delayed several hearings, as the system is often used for defendants who are in custody but need to attend court. Meanwhile, bank customers at high street lenders and leading digital bank Revolut were left in limbo as services including card payments, cash withdrawals and transfers were disrupted. By 5pm most Revolut services were still marked on its website as having problems, with only six out of 27 services listed, including support chat and internal transfers, said to be working. The City regulator, the Financial Conduct Authority, confirmed it was in touch with Google and seeking more information about the outage. The Bank of England’s Prudential Regulation Authority is also understood to be speaking with the tech firm. Halifax, owned by Lloyds Bank, said on X that some of its customers had been unable to log in via the app and online. Revolut said its app was down because of a “service disruption” affecting several services, but insisted that customers’ funds and data were safe. The outage was affecting new and current customers, hitting its sign-up, sign-in, card payment, transfer, ATM withdrawal and cryptocurrency services. In a statement, Revolut said: “We are currently experiencing a service disruption affecting multiple services. We can confirm that all our customers’ money and data are safe. We’ve been working to fix the issue since it was identified this morning and apologise to anyone affected and for any inconvenience caused.” A spokesperson for Lloyds originally said that customers were having issues with “certain online services” on Tuesday morning but that they were aware of “similar issues affecting other organisations”. By the afternoon, the banking group issued an update saying: “We understand the root issues have been resolved, so we expect to [have] all our services back to normal soon.” HSBC customers had also been complaining online that they were struggling to access their accounts. A spokesperson for the bank said: “Our services have returned to normal following earlier access issues. We continue to monitor the situation closely and thank customers for their patience.” This outage will be the first major test for the FCA and the Bank of England since the City watchdogs were handed powers to regulate Google and other rival services that have been supplying “critical” cloud services to UK financial firms this summer. Their response is already facing scrutiny by the influential Treasury committee of MPs, whose chair Meg Hillier wrote to the regulators asking for details about how they reacted and a sense of the total impact on the UK financial system. Hillier has asked for an initial response by 4 November. The property portal Rightmove was also reportedly affected by the Google cloud outage, and NewsThump, the satire and spoof news website, was struggling to load. Reports suggested Royal Mail and DPD had also suffered disruption. Google said: “We will publish an analysis of this incident once we have completed our internal investigation. We thank you for your patience while we’re working on resolving the issue.” An FCA spokesperson said: “We are aware of the incident affecting services provided by Google and are engaging with them and affected firms to understand the impact. “People need to be able to access their money and make payments, so we expect firms to fix issues quickly, keep their customers informed and ensure no one is left out of pocket.”'),
(24, 'Business', 'https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/21/uk-government-borrowing-rises-john-healey-budget', 'Heather Stewart', 'UK government borrowing rises to £24.5bn as John Healey prepares for budget', 'No headings found', 'Government paid £10.9bn in debt interest, the highest September figure since records began Business live – latest updates The Treasury ran a higher than expected budget deficit of £24.5bn last month as rising inflation pushed up the interest paid on government debt, increasing pressure on John Healey before his budget next week. Official figures show the government paid £10.9bn in debt interest: the highest September figure since records began, and only the eighth time the monthly number has been above £10bn. Some government bonds are directly linked to the retail prices index measure of inflation, with payments rising in line with it. RPI has increased in recent months as the Iran war has pushed up energy costs. The Office for National Statistics said this had added £4bn to debt interest costs in September alone. Total government debt is estimated to have hit £3tn for the first time – although as a share of the size of the economy it has fallen to 94%, from 95% a year ago. The £24.5bn monthly deficit on the public finances was up from £17bn in August, and £1.9bn higher than the independent Office for Budget Responsibility had forecast. In the financial year so far, public sector net borrowing is £97.8bn, the ONS said – £4.4bn lower than last year but still £6bn higher than the OBR expected. The data underlines the impact of the Iran war on the public finances, before the budget next Wednesday. Healey has been keen to stress his determination to stay within the fiscal rules set out by his predecessor as chancellor, Rachel Reeves. However, he is widely expected to accept a slimmer financial buffer, or “headroom”, against those rules than the £24bn Reeves had built up at the time of her spring forecast. Higher inflation tends to be good for tax revenues but it also squeezes how much government departments can achieve within fixed budgets. Receipts from income-related taxes were up more than 7% on the same month last year in September, and corporation tax by 9%, aided by stronger than expected economic growth – but these higher receipts failed to offset the rising debt interest bill. Sign up to Business Today Get set for the working day – we\'ll point you to all the business news and analysis you need every morning after newsletter promotion Martin Beck, the chief economist at the consultancy WPI Strategy, said the latest data was “an unwelcome reminder of the pressures facing the chancellor ahead of next week’s budget”. Healey is under pressure to provide relief from rapidly rising energy costs. Greg Thwaites, the interim chief economist at the Resolution Foundation, said the public finances figures suggested he would have to take a cautious approach. “The support must be temporary and targeted given the state of the public finances. Borrowing is already off track this year, debt has hit £3tn, and markets are closely scrutinising the government’s ability to keep on top of the public finances,” he said. The Conservative shadow chancellor, Andrew Griffith, said the figures showed Labour should do more to tackle the deficit. “Government borrowing of more than £3tn – the highest in history – and rising inflation show Labour has lost control of the economy,” he said.');
